## ARCHITECTURE

### FOLDER STRUCTURE

```
/app
  /api/v1
    /plans/route.ts
    /plans/[planId]/route.ts
    /plans/[planId]/semesters/route.ts
    /plans/[planId]/semesters/[semId]/courses/route.ts
    /plans/[planId]/semesters/[semId]/courses/[courseId]/route.ts
    /events/route.ts
    /events/[eventId]/route.ts
    /courses/route.ts                        (public)
    /courses/[courseId]/route.ts             (public)
  /dashboard/page.tsx
  /courses/page.tsx
  /plans/[planId]/page.tsx
  /calendar/page.tsx
  /api-docs/page.tsx
  layout.tsx / page.tsx
/components        — flat, PascalCase, e.g. Button.tsx, CourseCard.tsx
/hooks             — e.g. useAuth.ts, usePlans.ts
/lib
  firebase.ts          — client-side init only
  firebase-admin.ts    — server-side only, never imported in client components
  api-helpers.ts       — verifyAuth(), errorResponse(), successResponse()
/docs
/scripts
  scrape-courses.js
```

### Key Architecture Rules
- The frontend **never** calls Firestore directly. All data fetching goes through `/app/api/v1/`.
- API routes use the Firebase Admin SDK to verify JWTs and read/write Firestore.
- Pass Firebase JWT as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on all authenticated requests.
- Only unauthenticated routes: `GET /api/v1/courses` and `GET /api/v1/courses/:courseId`.

### Auth Flow
1. User logs in via Firebase Auth client SDK → receives JWT
2. JWT stored in React context (never localStorage)
3. Every authenticated API call includes `Authorization: Bearer <token>`
4. API route verifies token server-side via firebase-admin

