## DO'S & DON'TS

**DO:**
- Use Server Components for pages without client interactivity; `'use client'` only when needed.
- Verify Firebase JWTs server-side on every authenticated route.
- Write tests alongside features — not at the end.
- Use environment variables for all secrets. Commit `.env.example` with placeholders.
- Use `ics` for calendar export (client-side only).

**DON'T:**
- Import `firebase-admin` in any client component.
- Call Firestore directly from client components.
- Store JWT in `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`.
- Use the `/pages` directory or `src/` directory.
- Use `any` in TypeScript.
- Write custom CSS.
- Create a separate Express server.
- Deploy to Firebase Hosting (use Vercel).
- Use `useEffect` for data fetching.
- Hard-block students on prereq violations — soft-warn only.
- Live-scrape searchneu.com on user requests.
- Commit `.env.local` or any Firebase service account key.

---

## SECURITY

- Verify Firebase JWT before any Firestore operation on authenticated routes.
- Firestore rules: users can only read/write their own documents. `courses` is read-only for clients.
- Never log JWTs, user emails, or PII.
- Validate all request bodies before writing to Firestore.
- Generic auth error messages — never reveal which field was wrong.
- Run `npm audit` in CI; fail on high-severity vulnerabilities.

## ACCESSIBILITY

- All interactive elements must be keyboard navigable.
- All images/icons must have `alt` or `aria-label`.
- Use semantic HTML (`<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<button>`).
- Color alone must not convey meaning (e.g. prereq warnings need text, not just red).
- Minimum contrast ratio: 4.5:1 (WCAG AA).
